# ============================================================
#  Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): castle_halls.
#  Repeatable routine events for map visits to the castle's
#  public halls: courtyard, library, refectory, lecture tower,
#  great hall, infirmary, alchemy laboratory and job board.
#  Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.
# ============================================================

# ---------------- The Courtyard ----------------

@event courtyard_sundial_bench
time: afternoon
title: The Slow Sundial
place: courtyard
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: sun
tags: study
scene: A stone bench beside a weathered sundial in a flagstone courtyard, a student reading with their back to the dial.
text: {There's a bench by the old sundial, which runs eleven minutes slow.|Someone has scratched a tiny "+11" by the dial's noon. A fellow sufferer.|The sundial bench is yours by custom. A first-year asked if you came with it.} {season == "winter": Frost fills the hour lines, and the shadow skates across them.|{~Pigeons are holding a meeting on the gnomon.|A first-year checks a watch against it, horrified.|Someone has chalked a face where noon should be.}}
> left: Read until the shadow moves
  lore +small
  health -tiny
  say: {season == "winter": {~Your fingers go numb by page two. The chapter is worth it.|You read in gloves, turning pages with your teeth.}|{~You finish a chapter in the minutes the sundial owes you.|A pigeon reads over your shoulder, unimpressed.|The shadow crawls; you outpace it by three pages.}}
> right: Close your eyes a while
  mood +small
  renown -tiny
  say: {~You wake with a chalk moustache and no suspects.|You wake to a prefect clearing his throat an inch from your ear.|You wake rested and late, eleven minutes late by the dial.}

@event courtyard_saved_seat
time: afternoon
title: A Seat on the Rim
place: courtyard
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: group
tags: social
cast:
  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer) and rel(here) >= 0
scene: Two students sitting on the dry edge of a courtyard fountain, bags between them, pigeons circling overhead.
text: {{here} has claimed the dry stretch of the fountain rim and waves you over.|{here} is on your favourite stretch of the fountain rim, the one the sun reaches first, and waves you over.|Your stretch of the fountain rim is known now. {here} is sitting on it and shifts over without being asked.} {~Two towers' bells are arguing about the hour.|Pigeons circle like creditors.|{season == "winter": Your breath hangs between you.|Someone's lunch smells better than yours.}}
> left: Sit down and catch up
  social here
  if npc_is(corvin):
    say: {~Corvin tells you three stories, one of them true, and calls you "saint" when you don't ask which.|Corvin spots a prefect, palms something off the rim, and goes on talking as if nothing happened.}
  elif npc_is(nessa):
    say: {~Nessa interviews you about the weather, the soup and your love life, in that order, and writes down all three.|Nessa reads you tomorrow's headline and asks if it's too cruel. It is. She keeps it.}
  elif npc_is(bram):
    say: {~Bram lists every rule broken in the courtyard while you sit. Then, gruffly, he asks how your exams went.|Bram writes a demerit for a passing first-year, then tears it up when he thinks you aren't looking.}
  else:
    say: {~You trade the castle's news for an hour. {here} does most of the trading.|You talk until the shadows cross the fountain. Neither of you can remember about what.}
> right: Wave, and keep walking
  lore +small
  rel here -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {~You promise next time. {here} looks as if {they} {are} counting.|You spend the hour with a book instead. It is a good book; it is not much company.}

@event courtyard_seasonal_game
title: The Courtyard Game
place: courtyard
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: group
tags: social
scene: Students playing a rowdy game across a flagstone courtyard while others crowd the edges shouting bets.
text: {season == "winter": A snowball war between Emberhall and Stillwater has reached the treaty stage.|{season == "spring": A charmed ball is loose, dodging everyone who kicks at it.|{season == "summer": It's too hot for sport, so it's catch with a charmed apple that bites.|It's conker season. The champion conker is said to be older than the fountain.}}} {Someone shouts for one more player.|They shout for you by name.|Both sides shout for you, and start bidding.}
> left: Join in
  challenge duel using vigor easy:
    win:
      renown +small
      mood +small
      health -tiny
      say: {~Your side wins, and the result is disputed until supper.|You score the deciding point and are carried three whole steps before they drop you.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood +tiny
      say: {~You are flattened, politely, by both sides at once.|You spend the rest of the game as the thing the other side aims at.}
> right: Hold the coats and the bets
  chance 50:
    gold +small
    say: {~The favourite loses. You are the only person in the courtyard who is pleased about it.|The underdog wins by a whisker, and the coats you are holding are suddenly lighter on your conscience.}
  else:
    gold -small
    say: {~The favourite wins, and so does everyone who backed them, out of your pocket.|Everyone bet the same way, and everyone was right. You pay out in a hail of small coins.}

# ---------------- The Library ----------------

@event library_wandering_book
title: The Book That Moved
place: library
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: book
tags: study
scene: Tall library shelves leaning very slightly, a gap where one book should be, a student on a ladder peering into it.
text: {Yesterday your book was on the third floor. Today the third floor has never heard of it.|Your book has moved again, leaving a bookmark in the gap, like a note.|You keep a map of where the shelves hide your books. They've started hiding the map.} {~In its place is a shelf of cookery spells.|In its place are bound weather reports.|In its place is one slim volume, face out, waiting.} {final_year: Soon they'll play these games with someone else.|}
> left: Hunt it down
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {You find it on the sixth floor, filed under "regret".|You find it in the stairwell, halfway between floors, as if it had been trying to leave.|You find it at once. Either you are learning the shelves, or they are getting lazy.}
> right: Read what's in front of you
  lore +tiny
  mood +small
  renown -tiny
  say: {~You arrive at seminar having read the wrong book with great conviction.|You learn a great deal about something nobody will ever ask you about.|The wrong book turns out to be the right book for a question nobody asks until the exam.}

@event library_shared_table
title: The Last Free Chair
place: library
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: social study
cast:
  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer) and rel(here) >= 0
scene: A crowded library reading room, every table full but one, a single empty chair facing a student absorbed in a book.
text: {The library is full. The only free chair is opposite {here}.|The only free chair is opposite {here}. A shelf has leaned over it, as if keeping it for you.|The library knows your habits now. The free chair, opposite {here}, has a book you wanted waiting on it.} {~Rain ticks on the tall windows.|A shelf nearby is quietly rearranging itself.|Footnote is asleep in the returns basket.|{season == "winter": The radiators clank like a ghost with a grudge.|Dust turns gold in the light.}}
> left: Take the chair and work
  rel here +small
  lore +small
  if npc_is(kael):
    say: {~Kael doesn't look up. By the end of the hour there is frost on your inkwell and a tiny, careful correction in your margin.|Kael pushes a page of his notes across to you, then looks away as if it had moved on its own.}
  elif npc_is(lyra):
    say: {~Lyra slides the candle an inch toward your side without looking up. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra finishes before you, and waits, and says she wasn't waiting.}
  elif npc_is(seraphine):
    say: {~Seraphine writes a word in your notes that you won't understand until next week.|Seraphine reads the same page for an hour, smiling at something that isn't on it.}
  elif npc_is(bram):
    say: {~Bram informs you that you breathe too loudly. Then he lends you his ruler.|Bram underlines every rule in his book twice. Once, he asks your opinion of one.}
  else:
    say: {~You work in silence for an hour, which turns out to be a kind of company.|Neither of you speaks. Both of you get further than you would have alone.}
> right: Whisper a question
  rel here +small
  mood +small
  renown -tiny
  say: {~The conversation is worth it. The look from the front desk has a long memory.|You get three sentences in before a shush arrives from two floors up.}

@event library_overdue
title: Overdue
place: library
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: book
tags: study money
scene: A student holding a book behind their back near a wicker returns basket, a cat on the front desk watching them.
text: {The book you borrowed in a hurry is overdue, and the library knows it. The spine has started to sigh.|Another overdue book. The front desk has begun keeping a little card with your name on it.|Your overdue books sigh in chorus now, and the card at the front desk has a second side.} {~Footnote watches you approach the returns basket with professional interest.|The front desk is, for once, unattended.|A queue of first-years is paying fines in small, sad coins.}
> left: Own up and pay the fine
  alignment +tiny
  gold -small
  lore +small
  say: {The fine is paid; the desk, mollified, lends you the sequel.|You pay, and are lent another book, which you suspect is a trap.|The desk sighs, takes your coins and hands you a book it says you'll like. It is always right, which is the annoying part.}
> right: Slip it into the basket
  challenge deceive easy:
    win:
      guile +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {~The book goes in without a sound. It stops sighing at once, which feels like forgiveness.|You drop it in under cover of a sneeze. Footnote saw. Footnote will keep it to himself, for a price.}
    lose:
      gold -small
      renown -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {~The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. You pay double.|It lands with a groan. The front desk adds a mark to your card without looking up.}

# ---------------- The Refectory ----------------

@event refectory_late_supper
time: evening
title: Late Supper
place: refectory
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: meal
tags: home
scene: The far end of a long refectory table at night, most benches empty, a single student with a bowl and a half-written letter.
text: {You come in late. The benches are empty and the cooks are wiping the far tables.|The cook leaves a lamp lit at the end of the bench now, where the draught can't find you.|Your late bowl is waiting under a plate, with a spoon laid beside it. Nobody admits to it.} {year == 1: Home feels very far away tonight.|{final_year: You count the suppers you have left, and stop.|{~The candles are down to stubs.|Someone has left a half-done puzzle on the next table.}}}
> left: Write a letter home
  mood +small
  gold -tiny
  say: {~You write two pages and post them with the last of your coppers.|You write about the food. You do not write about anything else, but they'll know.|You write a letter that is mostly questions. You post it before you can make it braver.}
> right: Revise over the bowl
  lore +small
  say: {~You learn three dates and a spill of gravy across all of them.|The cook reads over your shoulder and corrects one of your answers. She is right.}

@event refectory_house_tables
time: evening
title: Where to Sit
place: refectory
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: group
tags: social
scene: A crowded refectory at suppertime, a student standing with a tray between a loud house table and a quieter one of strangers.
text: {Supper, and the question of where to sit.|Your house table has started saving a gap on the bench, roughly your width.|The gap is a named seat now, and the quiet table nods at you too.} Your house table is {~loud with a story about a goat|in the middle of a pudding treaty|singing, badly}. At the far end, {season == "winter": some second-years from Rootward huddle round a warming charm.|some second-years from Duskgate are arguing quietly about a map.}
> left: Squeeze in with your house
  mood +small
  renown +tiny
  say: {~You eat with an elbow in your ribs and laugh the whole way through.|Somebody's story gets better every time you hear it. So does the pudding.}
> right: Sit with the strangers
  charm +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {~They are polite, then curious, then actually rather good company.|It is awkward for exactly one course. By pudding, they have told you their secret map route.}

@event refectory_pudding_tax
time: evening
title: The Pudding Tax
place: refectory
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cup
tags: social
cast:
  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and (tagged(friend, student) or tagged(friend, prefect))
scene: Two friends at a refectory table, one guarding a bowl of treacle pudding, the other holding a spoon at the ready.
text: {{friend} sits down opposite you and looks at your pudding the way a cat looks at a fish.|Your pudding has a reputation now. {friend} sits down opposite you with a spoon already in hand.|Word of your pudding tax has got round the refectory. Tonight {friend} comes to collect, with a little receipt.} {season == "winter": Tonight it's treacle sponge, steaming.|{~Tonight it's apple crumble.|Tonight it's a custard that wobbles with intent.}}
> left: Pay the pudding tax
  rel friend +small
  mood -small
  if npc_is(tobias):
    say: {~Tobias apologises four times, eats it all, then tells you a long story about a goat who did the same. It helps, somehow.|Tobias eats half, then pushes the bowl back with a sprig of mint from his pocket on top, as an apology.}
  elif npc_is(lyra):
    say: {~"Efficient," Lyra says, finishing it. The next day a whole pudding appears on your tray. She doesn't mention it.|Lyra eats it in four exact bites and critiques the custard. She does not give it back.}
  elif npc_is(mira):
    say: {~Mir eats it while explaining a pudding-defence device that would probably work. Probably.|Mir eats it with a spoon they built themselves, which hums. They say the humming is probably normal.}
  else:
    say: {~{friend} eats exactly half and leaves you the burnt corner.|{friend} eats it slowly, savouring your defeat.}
> right: Defend your pudding
  challenge deceive easy:
    win:
      mood +small
      rel friend -tiny
      say: {~You hide it under a napkin and point at the window. It works. {friend} will remember this.|You claim it's been sneezed on. {friend} retreats in disgust, and you eat in peace.}
    lose:
      mood -tiny
      rel friend +tiny
      say: {~{friend} sees straight through you and takes the whole thing as a penalty. It is hard to stay cross.|{friend} distracts you with a story and the bowl is empty before it ends.}

# ---------------- The Lecture Tower ----------------

@event lecture_tower_old_board
title: Someone Else's Lecture
place: lecture_tower
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: scroll
tags: study
scene: An empty spiral lecture hall, a blackboard still dense with chalk diagrams from a class that has long gone.
text: {term: {The fourth-floor hall is empty, but someone else's lecture is still on the board.|In the empty hall, a stub of chalk waits on the ledge, as if for you.|The board has a corner fenced off in chalk, labelled "answers here".}|In the break the boards are wiped, except one nobody dares touch.} {~Today it's a ward diagram, labelled "wrong, but how?"|Today it's half a proof, ending in a thumbprint.|Today it's a list headed "on the exam", crossed out.}
> left: Copy it all down
  arcana +small
  say: {~Your hand cramps. Your head, for once, does not.|You copy it all and understand most of it. The rest you'll grow into.}
> right: Add your own answer
  chance 60:
    renown +small
    say: {~Next time, someone has added "yes!" under your line.|Someone has answered your answer. The board has become a correspondence.|By the afternoon someone has drawn a box around your line, to keep it safe.}
  else:
    renown -tiny
    mood -tiny
    say: {~The professor whose board it was finds you holding the chalk. "Hm," says the look.|Your answer is wiped by the time you reach the door, by someone who knew better.}

@event lecture_tower_stair_question
title: One Question a Flight
place: lecture_tower
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: tower
tags: study
cast:
  prof: thorne
scene: A narrow spiral stair inside a stone tower, a severe professor climbing ahead with a stack of papers, a student a step behind.
text: {You meet Professor Thorne on the spiral stair. He allows one question per flight, and climbs fast.|Professor Thorne on the stair. He has slowed down by about one step in seven, which is how you know he expects you.|Thorne hands you half his stack of papers at the foot of the stair, without a word, and starts climbing.} {term: |The tower is half empty for the break; your footsteps echo twice.}{final_year: There are not many of these climbs left.|}
> left: Ask a hard question
  challenge exam using arcana medium:
    win:
      arcana +small
      rel prof +tiny
      say: {~He answers in four words, and the four words are enough.|"Hm," he says, then answers it properly, all the way to the seventh floor.}
    lose:
      arcana +tiny
      mood -small
      say: {~He answers with a harder question and leaves you on the landing.|"Wrong question," he says. "Find the right one by Thursday."}
> right: Climb in silence beside him
  rel prof +small
  health -tiny
  say: {~He climbs faster than you. At the top he says, "Adequate," and you are fairly sure he means the climbing.|Neither of you says a word for seven flights. It is oddly restful, apart from your legs.}

@event lecture_tower_scorched_room
title: The Scorched Room
place: lecture_tower
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: flame
tags: magic danger
scene: A practice room on a lower floor of a stone tower, walls streaked with scorch marks, a sign by the door forbidding unsupervised casting.
text: {The practice room on the Evocation floor is empty. A sign forbids unsupervised casting.|The scorched room is empty. Since your last visit, someone has underlined the sign.|The scorched room. The sign is underlined three times now, and signed by the headmistress.} {~A fresh scorch mark is shaped like a surprised face.|Someone has left a bucket of sand by the door.|{season == "winter": The walls are the only warm thing here.|The air smells faintly of singed eyebrow.}}
> left: Practise anyway
  mana -small
  chance 70:
    arcana +small
    say: {~Your spell lands clean in the centre of the oldest scorch mark. You leave no evidence.|You practise until the flame does what you mean, not what you say.}
  else:
    health -small
    renown -tiny
    say: {~A new scorch mark joins the wall, shaped very like you.|The spell comes back at you. You leave with your eyebrows, mostly.}
> right: Work through the theory
  lore +small
  say: {~You draw the spell on paper fifty times. Paper is very forgiving.|You learn exactly why the walls look like that, and resolve not to add to them.}

# ---------------- The Great Hall ----------------

@event great_hall_tomorrow_sky
title: Tomorrow's Weather
place: great_hall
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: magic
scene: An empty great hall in the evening, floating candles drifting under a vaulted ceiling that shows a different sky from the windows.
text: {The great hall is empty but for the candles. The ceiling shows tomorrow.|One candle drifts over to you and hovers at reading height.|Your candle meets you at the door now and leads you to your bench.} {season == "winter": Tomorrow, it says, it will snow.|{season == "spring": Tomorrow, a storm with bad manners.|{season == "summer": Tomorrow is cloudless, stars and all.|Tomorrow is fog, rolling across the vaults.}}} {final_year: There are fewer tomorrows here now.|}
> left: Lie on a bench and watch
  mood +small
  health -tiny
  say: {~You watch tomorrow until your neck stiffens. It is worth it.|A candle drifts down to see if you are all right, and stays.|You fall half asleep and dream tomorrow a little early.}
> right: Read by the floating candles
  lore +small
  say: {~A candle drips wax onto the best paragraph. You read it anyway, through the wax.|The candles follow your page like curious moths. It is the best light in the castle.}

@event great_hall_choir_rehearsal
time: evening
title: The Choir Rehearses
place: great_hall
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: music
tags: social
scene: A choir rehearsing on the stage of a candlelit great hall, a few singers floating a foot off the boards, the benches mostly empty.
text: {The choir is rehearsing on the stage, a foot off the floor, and one voice short.|Still a voice short. The conductor has left a spare songbook on your bench.|The spare songbook has your name pencilled in it, and the alto line marked in red.} {season == "winter": They are learning the Midwinter music.|{~Tonight it's a round that nobody can find the end of.|Tonight it's a lament, sung far too cheerfully.}}
> left: Listen from the back
  mood +small
  gold -tiny
  say: {~The collection hat comes round. It is worth every coin.|You listen until the candles gutter. The hat comes round twice.}
> right: Fill the missing voice
  challenge negotiate using charm easy:
    win:
      renown +small
      charm +tiny
      say: {~You hit every note but one. The conductor says it was the best one.|You float a little off the floor on the last chord. Nobody mentions it, which is how you know.}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {~You come in confidently, in the wrong key, in the wrong song. The choir is very kind about it, which is worse.|You sing the round in a straight line and finish alone. The conductor thanks you for your enthusiasm.}

@event great_hall_high_table_dare
title: The High Table Dare
place: great_hall
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: crown
tags: social danger
scene: An empty high table on a dais at the end of a dark great hall, a carved chair at its centre, students daring each other from the doorway.
text: {Someone dares you to sit in the headmistress's chair at the empty high table.|The dare comes round again, and someone has started keeping a tally in chalk on the doorframe.|The tally on the doorframe has your column and everyone else's. First-years dare each other to dare you.} {~The chair is still warm.|A cushion embroidered with an owl sits on it.|Her teacup has been left behind, still steaming, which is worse.}
> left: Take the dare
  challenge deceive easy:
    win:
      renown +small
      mood +small
      say: {~You sit, cross your legs, and sip the air. The doorway erupts.|You sit for a full minute. The chair is surprisingly comfortable, and very slightly warm.}
    lose:
      renown -small
      say: {~As you sit, a quiet, delighted laugh comes from the dark beyond the dais. You leave very fast.|A portrait above the table clears its throat. You have never moved so fast in your life.}
> right: Tell them you value your life
  renown -tiny
  mood +tiny
  say: {~They call you a coward, fondly. You keep your dignity, and your place at the academy.|"Coward," they chant. You bow. It seems the safest thing to do in that hall.}

# ---------------- The Infirmary ----------------

@event infirmary_spare_bed
title: The Spare Bed
place: infirmary
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: bed
tags: home
scene: A row of white infirmary beds under a high arched window, one turned down, a stern healer pointing at it.
text: {You only came in for a plaster.|Your name is on the slate by the end bed, in pencil.|Your name on the slate is in ink now.} {health < 40: Sister Amaranth takes one look at you and points at the spare bed.|Sister Amaranth decides, over her spectacles, that you'll live.} {~It smells of lavender and singed wool.|Two beds down, someone hiccups in a minor key.|{season == "winter": A warming charm hums under every blanket.|The curtains breathe.}}
> left: Lie down for an hour
  mood +small
  health +small
  gold -tiny
  say: {~You sleep the deep, dreamless sleep of the infirmary. The tonic afterwards costs a crown and tastes of regret.|You wake under a blanket you didn't pull up. There is a tonic on the side, and a bill under it.}
> right: Help roll bandages
  alchemy +small
  say: {~Sister Amaranth teaches you the knot she uses. You will never tie your boots any other way.|You roll bandages and listen to her opinion of duelling, which is long and specific.}

@event infirmary_visiting_hour
title: Visiting Hour
place: infirmary
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cross
tags: social
cast:
  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and (tagged(friend, student) or tagged(friend, prefect))
scene: A friend propped up in an infirmary bed by a window, a visitor on a stool beside them with a paper bag of grapes.
text: {Visiting hour. {friend} is in the bed by the window,|Visiting hour. Sister Amaranth waves you to the stool you used last time. Today it's {friend},|Visiting hour. The stool by the window has worn to your shape. Today it's {friend},} {~nursing a duelling bruise the colour of plums.|recovering from a draught of hiccups that hiccups back.|wrapped in three blankets against a lake chill.} {They} brighten{s} when you come in.
> left: Sit and keep {them} company
  social friend
  say: {~You bring grapes and eat most of them yourself. {friend} doesn't mind.|You stay until Sister Amaranth sweeps you out. {friend} waves from the pillows.}
> right: Smuggle in a pastry
  chance 60:
    rel friend +small
    mood +tiny
    say: {~The pastry makes it past the desk. {friend} eats it under the blanket like a crime.|You hide the pastry in a hollowed-out book. {friend} is delighted by both.}
  else:
    rel friend +tiny
    renown -tiny
    say: {~Sister Amaranth confiscates the pastry and eats it herself, slowly, while looking at you.|Sister Amaranth smells the pastry from across the ward and holds out her hand without a word.}

@event infirmary_tonic_cupboard
title: The Tonic Cupboard
place: infirmary
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: potion
tags: danger
scene: A tall glass-fronted cupboard of coloured tonics in an empty infirmary, its door ajar, a teacup steaming on the healer's desk.
text: {The tonic cupboard is open, and Sister Amaranth is in the back having her tea.|The tonic cupboard is open at teatime. A new label on the door reads "counted daily".|At teatime the cupboard is open, and the label now reads "counted daily, by name". The bright blue bottles have moved to the front.} {mana < 20: Your head aches with the dry ache of spent magic.|{~The bottles clink in the draught.|A label reads, in her handwriting, "absolutely not".}}
> left: Help yourself to a tonic
  challenge deceive easy:
    win:
      mana +small
      health +tiny
      guile +tiny
      say: {~You drink it in the corridor. It tastes of peppermint and getting away with things.|You pocket the bottle, rinse it later, and put it back full of water. You feel slightly worse about that.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      renown -tiny
      say: {~"That one is for goats," says Sister Amaranth from the doorway. She makes you drink the right one, which is worse.|Sister Amaranth's teacup appears at your elbow. "Put it back," she says, "and sit down."}
> right: Ask for one properly
  gold -small
  health +small
  mana +tiny
  say: {She charges you, lectures you, and gives you the good one.|She charges you, lectures you a little less, and gives you the good one.|"You again," she says, and hands it over before you ask.}

# ---------------- The Alchemy Laboratory ----------------

@event alchemy_lab_spare_cauldron
title: A Spare Cauldron
place: alchemy_lab
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cauldron
tags: study magic
scene: A long laboratory bench in the afternoon light, a small cauldron free among larger ones, jars of ingredients lined up beside it.
text: {There is a spare cauldron on the second bench, unclaimed.|The spare cauldron has a chalk mark on its rim like your initial.|Your cauldron, everyone calls it. Someone has scratched your initial into the handle.} {~Someone has left a ladle standing upright in something.|The bench smells of mint and burnt sugar.|{season == "summer": The heat makes every brew twice as eager.|The windows have steamed over with other people's experiments.}}
> left: Brew a practice batch
  challenge brew easy:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {~The brew turns the right colour at the right moment. It feels like being trusted.|It comes out clear and smells of nothing, which is exactly right.}
    lose:
      alchemy +tiny
      health -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {~The brew turns a colour you have never seen. So do your eyebrows.|It foams over and sets, firmly, around your sleeve.}
> right: Scrub the cauldrons for crowns
  gold +tiny
  alchemy +tiny
  mood -small
  say: {~You learn more about failed potions from their residue than from any lecture.|You scrub until the bench is clean. Then you scratch your own initials into it, small, under the rim.}

@event alchemy_lab_third_floor
title: The Third-Floor Fumes
place: alchemy_lab
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: bottle
tags: social magic
scene: A crowded upper floor of an alchemy laboratory, coloured vapour hanging under the ceiling, students working with handkerchiefs tied over their faces.
text: {On the third floor the ventilation charm has given up, and the fumes have taken charge.|Someone has hung a sign by the stairs giving the fumes' mood of the day.|The fumes' sign has a column for forecasts now, in several hands.} {~Everyone is speaking in rhyme and trying to hide it.|Everyone is laughing at nothing and cannot stop.|Everyone is being unusually honest about everyone else's hair.} {season == "winter": The windows are frozen shut.|}
> left: Stay and work in the fug
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  say: {~You get a great deal done and agree with everyone about everything.|You leave with good notes and a headache that hums.}
> right: Force the windows open
  vigor +tiny
  renown +tiny
  say: {season == "winter": {~The ice cracks. Half the floor cheers; half complains of the cold.|The frozen latch gives, and so does your thumbnail.}|{~The windows give. The fumes leave, sulking, and the floor applauds.|You wedge the window with a textbook. The fumes drift out, and nearly the textbook too.}}

@event alchemy_lab_unlabelled_jar
title: The Unlabelled Jar
place: alchemy_lab
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: potion
tags: magic danger
scene: A dusty shelf of ingredient jars in an alchemy laboratory, one jar with no label glowing faintly, a student reaching for it.
text: {Someone has left an unlabelled jar on the shared shelf. It glows, faintly.|Another unlabelled jar on the shared shelf, beside the cautious label someone wrote for the last one.|The shared shelf now has a row of careful labels, and one new jar at the end of it, waiting its turn.} {~It smells of thunder.|Something inside it turns over when you look.|It is warm to the touch.} {gpa >= 3: You could probably identify it, given time.|You have no idea what it is.}
> left: Add a pinch to your brew
  mana -tiny
  chance 50:
    mana +medium
    alchemy +tiny
    say: {~The brew turns gold and so do you, briefly. You feel enormous.|The brew sings a single note and leaves you humming with power.}
  else:
    health -small
    say: {~The brew ignites, politely, and then impolitely.|Your cauldron sneezes. So, for the rest of the afternoon, do you.}
> right: Label it, carefully
  lore +small
  say: {~An hour of tests later, you write a cautious label. Someone adds a skull to it.|You work out what it is. Then you wash your hands three times.}

# ---------------- The Job Board ----------------

@event job_board_odd_jobs
title: Odd Jobs
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: paper
tags: job money
scene: A cork board in an entrance hall, a small notice pinned at an angle under the official job postings, a student reading it with a frown.
text: {Under the proper postings, someone has pinned an odd job.|Under the proper postings is an odd job, and a second pin someone has left beside it, for answers.|The odd-jobs corner has a card addressed to you by name, in a hand you're starting to recognise.} {~Someone wants a homesick portrait read to.|Someone wants a ladder held, no questions asked.|Someone wants a reluctant gargoyle walked.} {term: |In the break the board is half bare, and the odd jobs pay a little better.}
> left: Take the job
  gold +small
  mood -tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {~It takes all afternoon and pays exactly what it said, which is rarer than it should be.|It is stranger than the notice suggested, and pays slightly less.}
> right: Haggle first
  challenge negotiate easy:
    win:
      gold +small
      charm +tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {~You haggle the fee up by half. Your employer seems to respect you more for it.|You agree a price and a biscuit. The biscuit turns out to be the better half of the deal.}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {~Your employer takes the notice down and pins up a new one, asking for "someone less difficult".|The job goes to a first-year who asked for nothing. They seem very happy with it.}

@event job_board_lost_familiars
title: Lost and Found
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: cat
tags: social
scene: The corner of a cork board crowded with hand-drawn posters of lost familiars and a ransom note for a sock.
text: {The corner of the board for lost things is busier than the jobs.|An old poster in the lost-things corner now reads "found, thanks to a student".|The lost-things corner has thank-you notes now. One draws the rescuer with a halo.} {~A toad called "Sir" is missing, last seen being brave.|A ransom note demands three biscuits for one sock.|A small owl is lost, answers to nothing, and bites.} {season == "winter": Half the familiars have simply gone somewhere warm.|}
> left: Go looking for it
  chance 50:
    gold +small
    mood +small
    say: {~You find it behind a radiator in the east tower, deeply unrepentant. The reward is small and grateful.|You find it, and the owner cries, and the reward is mostly biscuits.}
  else:
    health -tiny
    mood -tiny
    say: {~You search for an hour and find only a different, much angrier creature.|You don't find it. The next day the poster says "Found! Thanks anyway."}
> right: Read every notice twice
  guile +small
  say: {~The board is the castle's diary. You learn who lost what, and who owes whom.|You learn three secrets from the small print, and who has terrible handwriting.}

# Job board stories (playtest 2, N13): small, everyday, plot-safe.

@event job_board_yellow_notice
title: The Oldest Notice
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: warm
weight: 10
art: paper
tags: job social
scene: A corner of a cork board where one notice, yellowed and curling, is pinned beneath years of newer ones.
text: In the bottom corner of the board, under years of newer notices, is one gone yellow and soft: "Wanted, Thursdays: someone to read the newssheet aloud to the portrait on the west stair. He likes the sport and the obituaries. Small pay." Someone has renewed the pin.
> left: Answer it, this Thursday
  gold +tiny
  mood +small
  study -tiny
  say: The portrait is a bald man in an old-fashioned collar who hasn't had the news read to him in years. He cheers the duelling results and sighs at the obituaries. You lose your Thursday evening, and he asks you to come back.
> right: Ask the porter who pins it
  lore +small
  say: The porter says the notice was there when he started, and that a few crowns appear on the stair every Thursday whether anyone reads or not. He has never asked who leaves it. He suggests you don't either.

@event job_board_torn_notice
title: Half a Notice
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: light
weight: 10
art: paper
tags: job money
scene: Two students tugging at either end of a single job notice in front of a crowded cork board, the paper starting to tear.
text: A well-paid notice ("one evening's portering, generous rates") has drawn two students, who have both got hold of it. It tears down the middle as you arrive. One holds the half with the pay; the other holds the half with the address. Both look at you.
> left: Settle it between them
  charm +small
  say: You suggest they share the evening and split the fee. They both hate the idea, then both agree to it, then both thank you. You get nothing but a reputation for being reasonable.
> right: Read the address and go yourself
  gold +small
  alignment -tiny
  renown -tiny
  say: You read the address over a shoulder and get there first. The job pays well. By supper both of them have told everyone what you did.

@event job_board_monthly_letter
title: A Letter a Month
place: job_board
class: routine
tone: warm
weight: 10
art: letter
tags: job social
scene: A small handwritten notice pinned beside a sealed letter on a cork board, a student reaching to unpin both.
text: A small notice in a careful, shaky hand: "Wanted: a student to post a letter for me in the village, first of each month. A few crowns. I can't manage the hill any more." The letter is pinned beside it, sealed and addressed to someone in the village.
> left: Take the letter down the hill
  gold +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: You post it, then the next, then the next. In the third month the letter-writer leaves a note: the replies have started coming. You never learn who either of them is. You keep the crowns in a jar.
> right: Leave it for someone else
  say: You leave it on the board. The next morning it's gone, and someone has drawn a small heart beside the notice in pencil.
